   The polytope $\mathcal A_\theta$ is nonempty and compact. Nonemptiness follows from the product of the input law and the empirical residual law; compactness follows from the fixed row sums and nonnegativity. Both finite primal objectives are bounded between zero and one. Finite-dimensional LP strong duality therefore equates their values with their dual optima \citep{B.V04}.
 
   It remains to identify these finite duals with the measure duals. For fixed multipliers and $\bm x\in A_i$, the upper pointwise constraints are equivalent to
-  $\varphi(\bm x)\ge\max_j(\overline h_{ij}-\psi_j)$.
+  $\varphi(\bm x)\ge\max_j(\overline h_{ij}-\eta_j)$.
   Their least feasible potential is constant on each $A_i$ and can be written as $z_i$. This gives \eqref{eq-finite-upper-dual} exactly. Similarly, the lower constraints are equivalent to
-  $\varphi(\bm x)\le\min_j(\underline h_{ij}+\psi_j)$,
+  $\varphi(\bm x)\le\min_j(\underline h_{ij}+\eta_j)$,
   yielding \eqref{eq-finite-lower-dual}. The resulting potentials are bounded and measurable. Together with Lemma~\ref{lem-exact-aggregation}, these identities establish strong duality for the measure programs.
